@@ -485,6 +485,25 @@ in-use bitmap followed by 128 channels of 56 bytes (0x1C10 bytes per bank), so c
 0x7B1C0 + 16 x ((n-129) / 128) + 56 x (n-129). Bitmaps: bit (k % 8) of byte k / 8 marks the bank's channel k (0-based)
 in use. The flash image from 0x7B000 to 0x7B1AF is unused by the firmware; keep it as read.
 
+#### 7.3.3.2 FM signaling ID (multi-mode derivative)
+
+An FM channel has no use for the per-channel DMR ID. With flag 1 bit 7 set and bit 7 of 0x27 set, those three bytes
+are the ID the channel identifies itself with on air (`radio-features.md` 3.8):
+
+| Offset | Bits | Field |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 0x27 | 7 | 1: a signaling ID (no DMR ID reaches 0x800000) |
+| 0x27 | 6 | Decode the IDs received on the channel (stored; not used yet) |
+| 0x27 | 5 | Send the ID when PTT is released |
+| 0x27 | 4 | Send the ID when the transmission starts |
+| 0x27 | 3-0 | ID type: 0 none, 1 MDC-1200. A type the firmware doesn't know reads as none |
+| 0x28 | | The ID, high byte (MDC-1200: the unit ID, 0x0001-0xFFFF; 0 = none, nothing is sent) |
+| 0x29 | | The ID, low byte |
+
+There is no radio-wide ID for these: a channel without one sends none. With bit 7 of 0x27 clear the bytes are a DMR
+ID left from another mode, which FM ignores and keeps. A CPS that doesn't know this shows a per-channel DMR ID
+above 8388607 on an FM channel.
+
